git commit -m "The nine authorable Customer rules written, two new rule types to express them, and the multi-sheet question CW #1 was waiting on. CW_Issues rows 15 to 24 were all marked Authorable: the engine could express each one and nobody had typed it, because the source column spellings come from the analyst's extract and a wrong guess ships silently in a file that looks correct. On the 30-Jul instruction all nine are now authored as data, which is the same destination and the same precedence as typing them into the rule box. Two things make pre-filling them safe. Every source column is a LIST of candidate spellings rather than one guess: COALESCE skips a column that is absent or blank and a CASE_WHEN branch on an absent column reads as blank and falls through, so naming six plausible spellings binds to whichever the extract uses and costs nothing when wrong, whereas one guessed spelling binds to nothing and fails quietly. And nothing overwrites a real source value: SEQUENCE keeps a source key when there is one, CONCAT falls back rather than emitting a bare separator, and every CASE_WHEN default is either the stated fallback or blank, never an invented code. Two rule types are new because nothing existing could express them. SUFFIX_WHEN appends one of several suffixes chosen per row, for CW #19's _b on a bill-to row and _s on a ship-to row; SUFFIX can only append a fixed string and CASE_WHEN can only replace a value, so this otherwise needed two chained rules per field that the analyst then has to keep in step. It is idempotent, because a key that gains a second _b on every generation is a new and silent data defect. SEQUENCE covers CW #23's NXT000001 with a _C1 form for a PERSON. CW #23 deserves its own note. Section 10.6 removed auto-generated key numbers on the analyst's own earlier instruction, because a manufactured unique value makes genuine duplicates look distinct and they then load twice; the 30-Jul instruction reverses that. Two guards keep the old problem from returning and both are tested: a real source key always wins, and the number is derived from the ROW INDEX rather than a counter so a re-run does not renumber every party and orphan the references the other 18 Customer sheets carry. Party Number must never be added to the duplicate-identity columns. Also flagged rather than silently corrected: as written the sheet gives 3 plus 6 digits for an organization but 3 plus 5 digits plus _C1 for a person, which is implemented exactly as specified and looks like a transcription slip. CW #17 was specified as depending on which source column fed Phone Number. Provenance is not decidable per row, so it is implemented on the per-row evidence instead: the mobile column carrying a value means MOBILE, the fax column means FAX, and a row with both is a mobile with a fax on file. Its default is blank rather than a guess, because Phone Line Type is a coded column and an invented code fails the value-set check with no clue where it came from. CW #1 is settled. A multi-sheet workbook has two opposite readings and the file cannot say which: several sources of the same record, which wants one conversion per sheet converged at generation, or one input split across sheets such as Customer and Address, which wants a single conversion over the union of the columns. The upload now ASKS, and asks with the evidence: which columns could join the sheets, how far their values actually overlap, and whether the row grains match, with a suggested answer and the reason for it, or an explicit statement that the file is ambiguous rather than a suggestion dressed as evidence. Answering one-input joins the sheets on a shared key into a single dataset. The join is on a key, never on row order: stacking two entity sheets side by side attaches one customer's address to another and the result looks immaculate. The key is detected and scored on whether the values genuinely overlap, so a column called City present in both sheets with no shared values is rejected while a legitimate one-to-many key that repeats on the address side is not penalised out of contention. The finer-grained sheet leads the join so no rows are dropped, colliding column names are kept under both names so a mapping cannot end up pointed at a silently overwritten column, duplicate keys on the joined side are collapsed and counted, a key that matched under half the rows raises a warning, and when no column joins the sheets at all the merge is refused with the reason instead of being performed. A refusal costs a conversation; a bad join costs a cutover. 431 tests over 36 files, run on the pinned pandas 2.2.3 and on 3.0.2."
